---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MergePdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/MergePdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="merge-pdf">
  <MergePdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to merge PDF files</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Drop all the PDFs you want to merge onto the box above, or click it to pick files. You can
          add more files later, and the files already in the list stay.
        </li>
        <li>
          Put them in order. Drag a row by the handle on the left, use the up and down buttons (they
          work with the keyboard), or press <strong>Sort A→Z</strong> for filename order.
        </li>
        <li>
          To use only some pages of a file, type a range such as
          <code class="font-mono">1-3, 8, 12-</code> in the Pages box next to it. Leave it empty to
          use the whole document.
        </li>
        <li>Press <strong>Merge</strong> and download the single combined PDF.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The whole process runs in this browser tab with WebAssembly. Nothing is uploaded, so a merge of
        a hundred megabytes of scans is as private as opening them in a desktop reader. There is no
        queue and no page cap.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Page range syntax</h2>
      <p>
        All range boxes on this site use the same shorthand. Pages count from 1 and duplicates are
        ignored, so <code class="font-mono">2, 2-4</code> gives pages 2, 3 and 4.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[480px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">You type</th>
              <th scope="col">You get</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono">(empty)</td>
              <td>Every page of the file</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono">5</td>
              <td>Page 5 only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono">1-3</td>
              <td>Pages 1, 2 and 3</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono">10-</td>
              <td>Page 10 to the last page</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono">-4</td>
              <td>The first four pages</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono">9-5</td>
              <td>Pages 9, 8, 7, 6, 5 (reversed)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono">odd / even</td>
              <td>All odd or all even pages</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="font-mono">last</td>
              <td>The final page</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What merging does and does not keep</h2>
      <p>
        The tool copies pages at the object level: the same fonts, vector paths and image streams.
        Nothing is rendered again, so a merged file looks identical to its sources, and text stays
        selectable and searchable.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Kept: page content, embedded fonts, images, page size and rotation, link and comment annotations, filled form-field appearances.</li>
        <li>Rebuilt: the document catalogue, page tree and cross-reference table. So the merged file is usually slightly smaller than the sum of its parts.</li>
        <li>Not kept: document-level bookmarks (outlines), attachments, JavaScript actions and digital signatures. A signature covers only the file it was applied to, so any merge invalidates it.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Common uses</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Combine a scanned contract, its annexes and an ID copy into one submission file.</li>
        <li>Put a month of receipts or invoices into one expense claim.</li>
        <li>Build a portfolio from separately exported chapters, with only the pages you want from each.</li>
        <li>Rebuild a document that a scanner split into several files, in the correct order.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If the merged file is larger than an upload limit, run it through the compressor afterwards.
        For scanned pages, a render at 110 DPI usually removes most of the size.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
